This paper presents a comprehensive comparative analysis of LMS-based online learning and traditional classroom education, examining key dimensions such as accessibility, interactivity, assessment methods, and resource utilization. The study traces the historical evolution of educational technologies—from 19th-century correspondence courses to radio and television broadcasts, early computer-assisted instruction like the PLATO system, and the transformative impact of the internet—culminating in today’s sophisticated Learning Management Systems. It emphasizes how digital platforms have redefined educational access and delivery, particularly through asynchronous and synchronous learning models, blended approaches, MOOCs, and adaptive learning systems that personalize instruction using data analytics.

The central focus lies in comparing modern LMS-facilitated online learning with conventional face-to-face instruction. Online learning, powered by systems like Moodle, Canvas, Google Classroom, and Brightspace, offers significant advantages in flexibility and reach. Learners can access materials anytime and anywhere, across devices, breaking down geographical and temporal barriers. This is especially beneficial for non-traditional students, working professionals, and those in remote or underserved regions. In contrast, traditional learning remains constrained by physical presence, fixed schedules, and infrastructure limitations, which can hinder equitable access.

In terms of engagement, LMS platforms enhance interactivity through multimedia content, discussion forums, virtual simulations, and collaborative tools such as real-time document editing and video conferencing. These features promote active learning and peer interaction, though they may lack the immediacy of in-person feedback. Traditional classrooms excel in direct instructor-student communication, spontaneous discussions, and hands-on activities—particularly valuable in disciplines requiring lab work or performance-based skills. However, online environments increasingly replicate these experiences through virtual labs and interactive modules.

Assessment methods also differ significantly. LMS-based systems support diverse and efficient evaluation tools, including automated quizzes with instant feedback, digital assignments, discussion participation tracking, and proctored online exams. These enable timely, data-driven insights into student performance. Traditional assessments rely more on written exams, oral presentations, practical demonstrations, and portfolio reviews—methods that allow deep qualitative evaluation but are often more time-consuming to administer and grade.

From a resource perspective, digital education proves more sustainable and scalable. By leveraging open educational resources and cloud-based content, institutions reduce dependency on printed materials, lower distribution costs, and minimize environmental impact. Online courses can scale to thousands of learners without additional physical infrastructure. Conversely, traditional education demands ongoing investment in textbooks, classroom materials, facilities, and logistical support, often resulting in higher operational costs and limited reusability.

The authors conclude that while traditional learning retains strengths in personal interaction and experiential learning, LMS-enabled online education offers a dynamic, inclusive, and future-ready alternative. It supports lifelong learning, accommodates diverse learner needs, and aligns with global trends toward digital transformation in education. They advocate for strategic integration of both models—particularly through blended learning—to maximize pedagogical effectiveness. Future advancements in AI, virtual reality, and adaptive learning technologies are expected to further bridge existing gaps, making online education even more immersive and personalized. Overall, the study underscores the transformative role of LMS platforms in shaping equitable, efficient, and student-centered educational ecosystems.